## Founding & History
Loira Vines was established in 2017 when Adrian and Mirabai Carruthers purchased the vineyard during a holiday wine tour in Tasmania. The name "Loira" is derived from an Aboriginal word meaning "charcoal reduced to powder". The area where the property is located was historically known as "Port Station" and was initially intended to be the town of Exeter, but development did not proceed due to flooding risks.

## People
Loira Vines is owned and operated by Adrian and Mirabai Carruthers, a husband-and-wife team. Mirabai has a background in primary school teaching, while Adrian previously managed a family pub for 14 years in the Hunter region. They relocated to Tasmania from Gloucester, New South Wales. The couple has received assistance from a professional winemaker as they gained experience in winemaking.

## Vineyard & Site
Loira Vines is a small, boutique vineyard spanning five and a half acres (approximately 2.2 hectares). The vineyard is situated on the West Tamar Highway, just north of Exeter. The grapevines are mature, with some being over 20 years old.

The varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Shiraz (Syrah), Red Frontignac, and Fragola. In addition to grapes, the vineyard cultivates Golden Delicious and Pink Lady apples for craft ciders, and four varietals of hops for beer production. The farming approach emphasizes careful hand-nurturing of grapes throughout the growing and winemaking process, aiming for minimal intervention in winemaking by focusing on vineyard work.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door is open on Friday, Saturday, and Sunday from 11 am to 5 pm. It can also be opened on other weekdays by appointment. Loira Vines is closed during the month of July, as well as on Christmas Day, Boxing Day, and New Year's Day. Visitors can enjoy wine tastings and gin paddles. The experience is described as warm and cosy in winter, and in summer, guests can enjoy afternoons by the dam where a platypus has been sighted. The vineyard features beautiful gardens centered around a lake, home to an elusive platypus, native hens, and various bird species.

Facilities at the cellar door include air conditioning, parking, and suitability for functions. It is also family-friendly, has a lawn/gardens, an outdoor dining area, and offers takeaway options. Pet-friendly visits are possible upon enquiry. The winery provides multiple booking options via web, email, and phone, and its staff are trained in disability awareness, accepting the Companion Card.

## Food & Hospitality
Loira Vines offers local produce platters that highlight artisanal products from Launceston and the surrounding Tamar Valley region. These platters feature items such as cheeses, charcuterie, condiments, sourdough, and fresh produce. Platters are available on Friday, Saturday, and Sunday from 11 am to 2:30 pm, and bookings are essential. The winery can cater to dietary requirements, though confirmation of numbers is crucial as preparation begins the night before. A vegan platter offered by Loira Vines has been noted for its quality, including four vegan cheeses, local chutneys, chocolate, fruit, bread, house-made dips, pumpkin, and pickles. For group functions, the venue can accommodate up to 50 guests for events such as birthday parties, dinner parties, and canapés.

## Awards & Recognition
No reliable information found from public sources.

## Sustainability & Certifications
No specific certifications for organic or biodynamic practices were found. The winery's approach involves carefully tending to its five and a half acres of vines and apples. Grapes are nurtured by hand, with a philosophy of minimal intervention during the winemaking process, assuming thorough work in the vineyard.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Loira Vines is 3191 West Tamar Highway, Sidmouth, Tasmania, 7270, Australia. The locality is Loira, approximately 7 minutes north of Exeter and about 31 kilometers northwest of Launceston. The primary contact phone number is 0418 477 760. The winery's website is http://www.loiravines.com.au/, and their email is taste@loiravines.com.au. Loira Vines maintains a presence on social media, with a Facebook page (https://www.facebook.com/WestTamarCellarDoor/) and an Instagram account (https://www.instagram.com/loira_vines/?hl=en).

## Notable / Distinctive
Loira Vines is recognized as one of the smallest vineyards in the Tamar Valley. It is a family-run operation managed by a husband-and-wife team. Beyond wines, the winery also produces craft ciders, pale ale, and unique gins. A distinctive feature is their focus on local produce platters, which showcase artisan producers from the region. All alcoholic beverages offered, including wines, ciders, and gins, are vegan. The property is noted for its dam where platypus sightings are known to occur. The name "Loira" itself has an Aboriginal origin, meaning "charcoal reduced to powder". Additionally, the property offers accommodation.

